latest_plan = 'Based on the reflection, here\'s a step-by-step plan to solve the challenge:\n\n1. Grid Analysis:\n   a. Analyze the input grid for color distribution and frequency.\n   b. Identify regions of interest by looking for areas with unique color patterns or high color diversity.\n   c. Detect any repeating patterns or symmetries in the grid (horizontal, vertical, and diagonal).\n\n2. Pattern Identification:\n   a. Implement a sliding window approach to scan the grid at various sizes (e.g., 3x3, 4x4, up to 9x9).\n   b. For each window, calculate a "significance score" based on factors like color diversity, uniqueness of pattern, and how well it represents the overall grid.\n   c. Keep track of the top-scoring windows and their locations.\n\n3. Output Size Determination:\n   a. Analyze the dimensions of the top-scoring patterns.\n   b. Consider the overall size of the input grid.\n   c. Determine an appropriate output size that balances representing the pattern and maintaining a reasonable reduction from the input size.\n   d. Ensure the output size is within the observed range (minimum 3x3, maximum 9x9).\n\n4. Pattern Extraction and Transformation:\n   a. Extract the highest-scoring pattern that fits the determined output size.\n   b. If necessary, transform the pattern (e.g., rotate, flip) to best represent the input grid\'s characteristics.\n\n5. Output Grid Construction:\n   a. Create a new grid with the determined output size.\n   b. Fill the grid with the extracted and possibly transformed pattern.\n   c. If the pattern is smaller than the output size, repeat or extend it logically to fill the grid.\n\n6. Validation and Adjustment:\n   a. Verify that the output grid contains at least 3 distinct colors.\n   b. If not, intelligently introduce additional colors from the input grid to ensure diversity.\n   c. Check that the output grid is a fair representation of the input grid\'s color distribution and patterns.\n\n7. Final Output:\n   a. Convert the constructed grid into a ColoredGrid object.\n   b. Return the final ColoredGrid as the solution.\n\n8. Testing and Refinement:\n   a. Run the solution against all provided test cases.\n   b. Analyze any discrepancies or unexpected results.\n   c. Refine the algorithm, particularly the significance scoring and pattern selection processes, based on test results.\n\n9. Edge Case Handling:\n   a. Implement specific handling for edge cases, such as very uniform or very chaotic input grids.\n   b. Ensure the algorithm degrades gracefully for inputs that don\'t have clear patterns.\n\n10. Optimization:\n    a. Review the implementation for performance bottlenecks, especially in the grid analysis and pattern identification steps.\n    b. Optimize critical sections to ensure the solution runs efficiently for 30x30 input grids.\n\nThis plan provides a comprehensive approach to solving the challenge, addressing the complexities observed in the reflection and aiming to create a solution that works across all provided examples and potential inputs.'
